WEBVTT

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:03.190
Read the source column from older rocks at the bottom toward

00:00:03.190 --> 00:00:04.350
younger rocks at the top.

00:00:04.550 --> 00:00:07.370
A fossil found through many beds has a broad range.

00:00:07.610 --> 00:00:10.790
Another confined to a short interval constrains age more tightly.

00:00:11.090 --> 00:00:15.990
A useful index fossil also needs abundance, wide distribution and recognizable

00:00:15.990 --> 00:00:18.550
features so it can be identified across sites.

00:00:19.350 --> 00:00:23.410
Two fossils found together must share a time interval, assuming they

00:00:23.410 --> 00:00:25.190
were not reworked from older deposits.

00:00:25.450 --> 00:00:29.050
Slide their known ranges together and retain only the overlap.

00:00:29.350 --> 00:00:32.490
The shared interval can be shorter than either fossil range by

00:00:32.490 --> 00:00:36.590
itself. Adding their full durations would discard the constraint instead of

00:00:36.590 --> 00:00:37.030
using it.

00:00:37.690 --> 00:00:41.550
The two source columns contain matching fossils but also disconformities.

00:00:41.770 --> 00:00:44.990
Some beds were eroded or never deposited at a site.

00:00:45.190 --> 00:00:48.330
A missing fossil can also reflect an unsuitable environment.

00:00:48.570 --> 00:00:52.050
Match supported horizons across the columns and mark the gaps;

00:00:52.250 --> 00:00:55.270
do not force every layer or fossil into both sequences.

00:00:56.290 --> 00:01:00.270
The second comparison has overlapping units four, five and six.

00:01:00.530 --> 00:01:02.710
The left section extends into older units;

00:01:03.010 --> 00:01:04.770
the right extends into younger ones.

00:01:05.050 --> 00:01:09.070
Match the overlap, then assemble a composite sequence from one through

00:01:09.070 --> 00:01:13.150
nine. Thickness can vary between sites, so matching ages does not

00:01:13.150 --> 00:01:14.790
require identical layer thickness.

00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:19.280
William Smith recognized recurring fossils in the same rock units while

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:22.700
surveying, even where faults and folds interrupted their exposure.

00:01:22.980 --> 00:01:27.100
Systematic faunal succession made correlation and geological mapping possible.

00:01:27.560 --> 00:01:30.820
Stratigraphy combines rock types, fossil evidence and environments;

00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:34.560
a convincing correlation explains both the matches and the differences.
